1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,226
Two series, driven by the same shocks. One is a random walk: every

2
00:00:04,326 --> 00:00:08,815
shock stays in it for ever. The other forgets each shock, very slowly,

3
00:00:08,915 --> 00:00:13,403
at rate zero point nine nine nine. For three hundred periods they draw

4
00:00:13,503 --> 00:00:17,271
what is practically one line. Which one is the random walk?

5
00:00:20,366 --> 00:00:22,583
This is the problem of testing for a unit

6
00:00:22,683 --> 00:00:25,125
root. The twins will stay with us throughout.

7
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:30,606
Standing at the last observation, we forecast three thousand periods

8
00:00:30,706 --> 00:00:34,649
ahead. The random walk's forecast is flat: today's value, for

9
00:00:34,749 --> 00:00:38,427
ever. The other forecast decays like zero point nine nine

10
00:00:38,527 --> 00:00:41,277
nine to the h, and slides back to its mean.

11
00:00:41,566 --> 00:00:45,552
Its half-life is about six hundred and ninety three periods. So

12
00:00:45,652 --> 00:00:49,573
the twins agree on next year and disagree completely about the

13
00:00:49,673 --> 00:00:53,464
next century. That is the whole difficulty: the data live in

14
00:00:53,564 --> 00:00:56,513
the left corner, the answer lives on the right.

15
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:01,272
Under the unit root, the least squares estimate minus

16
00:01:01,372 --> 00:01:04,112
one is a ratio. Its numerator hides a picture.

17
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:09,500
Square the walk at its end point and the cross terms rearrange

18
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:15,250
themselves: the numerator is half the difference between two squares. Yellow: the

19
00:01:15,350 --> 00:01:19,153
end point squared. Grey: the sum of all squared shocks.

20
00:01:19,433 --> 00:01:23,290
Draw six random walks. Most of the time the end point squared

21
00:01:23,390 --> 00:01:27,376
is the smaller square, so the numerator is negative and rho hat

22
00:01:27,476 --> 00:01:30,879
falls below one, even though the truth is exactly one.

23
00:01:30,966 --> 00:01:34,851
In the limit this happens with probability zero point six

24
00:01:34,951 --> 00:01:38,416
eight. Rho hat is biased downwards, and leans left.

25
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,214
So the usual t ratio cannot be compared with the bell curve. Watch

26
00:01:45,314 --> 00:01:49,921
its true distribution build up, one simulated random walk at a time. The

27
00:01:50,021 --> 00:01:54,170
histogram settles to the left of the bell, with a long left tail.

28
00:01:54,266 --> 00:01:57,929
The bell's five percent point is minus one point six four five;

29
00:01:58,029 --> 00:02:01,872
the Dickey Fuller five percent point is minus one point nine four.

30
00:02:01,972 --> 00:02:05,336
Add a constant to the regression and it moves to minus two

31
00:02:05,436 --> 00:02:09,040
point eight six; add a trend, minus three point four one. Each

32
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:12,504
deterministic term pushes the critical value further left.

33
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:18,728
Ask the test about the twins. With three hundred observations it rejects the random

34
00:02:18,828 --> 00:02:23,453
walk about five percent of the time, as designed. Against the twin with rho

35
00:02:23,553 --> 00:02:27,989
equal to zero point nine nine nine it also rejects about five percent of

36
00:02:28,089 --> 00:02:32,147
the time: the power of the test equals its size. Even rho equal to

37
00:02:32,247 --> 00:02:35,233
zero point nine five is missed four times in ten.

38
00:02:35,933 --> 00:02:38,546
This is not a defect of one test. It is in

39
00:02:38,646 --> 00:02:42,228
the problem. When we fail to reject, we learn that shocks

40
00:02:42,328 --> 00:02:45,394
are very persistent, not that rho is exactly one.

41
00:02:46,466 --> 00:02:49,463
So: under a unit root the estimate of rho leans to

42
00:02:49,563 --> 00:02:53,490
the left, and the t ratio follows the Dickey Fuller distribution,

43
00:02:53,590 --> 00:02:56,711
a functional of Brownian motion, not the bell curve.

